<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="011" project="spaarkeai-compose-r8">
  <metadata>
    <title>FR-S02 — Last-writer-wins with a warning; retire the 412 refusal loop</title>
    <phase>1 Track S — Save Reliability (P0, ships alone)</phase>
    <gate>blocked-until-010</gate>
    <status>pending</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Changes save-path concurrency semantics across server and client; reverses a behavior shipped to dev 2026-08-18; data-integrity blast radius.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Concurrency semantics + a deliberate reversal of a recent shipped decision; requires judgment about what "warn" means when a user overwrites a newer version.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Touches ComposeService.SaveAsync and ComposeWorkspace.tsx — the two most contended files in the project.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>010</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, frontend, compose, save-path, concurrency</tags>
    <estimated-effort>4-6 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    R7's UAT-25/26 fix (commit 6a75eb7b7, deployed 2026-08-18) made the mainstream ContentModel save refuse with
    HTTP 412 whenever the live SPE eTag differs from the Compose stamp. Combined with the dead client handler
    (task 010), this produced an UNRECOVERABLE LOOP: no reload path, and `state.etag` only updates on
    saveSucceeded, so every retry re-412s until a full page reload. It also compares eTag (not cTag), so
    metadata-only changes and Word-for-web sessions can trigger FALSE-POSITIVE lockouts.

    Owner decision 2026-08-19: concurrency is LAST-WRITER-WINS WITH A WARNING. Replace the refusal.
  </prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Expert in ComposeService.SaveAsync, SPE eTag/cTag semantics, and the Redis version-stamp path (ADR-009). Follow ADR-049 and ADR-007 strictly.</role>

  <goal>
    A save that lands on a moved base SUCCEEDS and warns the user that they overwrote a newer version, naming
    SPE version history as the recovery path. No save-path state can produce an unrecoverable retry loop. The
    412 refusal introduced 2026-08-18 is removed from the ContentModel path.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      design.md §3 S-2 and §7 D5. This supersedes the 412 behavior; ADR-049's third amendment (task 060) codifies
      last-writer-wins as invariant (e). Task 012 adds If-Match at the storage boundary, which is the DURABLE
      enforcement — this task removes the application-level refusal and adds the honest warning. The eTag-vs-cTag
      distinction matters: Graph eTags move on metadata-only changes, so an eTag comparison over-fires.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="primary-edit">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs</file>
      <file role="primary-edit">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeWorkspace.tsx</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ComposeEndpoints.cs</file>
      <file role="reference-impl">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeBannerStack.tsx</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-049">The save MUST terminate in a defined outcome (R8 standing invariant a). A stale base is NOT an undefined content-refusal — it is `persisted-with-warnings`. Scope: the ContentModel save path in SaveAsync.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-009">Version-stamp state MUST use IDistributedCache, never IMemoryCache. Scope: any stamp read/write touched here.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">MUST NOT simply delete the staleness DETECTION — detection is what powers the warning. Only the REFUSAL is removed.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">MUST assess eTag vs cTag and record the finding. If eTag over-fires on metadata-only changes, prefer cTag (content tag) for the "did the content actually move" signal. Scope: the staleness comparison only.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">MUST verify no retry path can loop: after a warned save, client state MUST advance so an immediate second save does not re-warn for the same event.</constraint>
    <constraint source="bff-extensions">State the Placement Justification in the PR and report publish size vs the 44.96 MB baseline (root §10).</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>compose save concurrency, SPE eTag, version stamping</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-049-compose-shadow-document.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/spec.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/CLAUDE.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r7/notes/uat-issues.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Rigor declaration">Declare rigor FULL; load spec.md, CLAUDE.md, ADR-049.</step>
    <step order="1">Locate the staleness assert added by 6a75eb7b7 in ComposeService.SaveAsync (~:1227) and trace exactly which request shapes reach it.</step>
    <step order="2">Determine empirically whether eTag or cTag is the correct "content moved" signal; record the finding in task notes.</step>
    <step order="3">Replace the refusal with: proceed with the write, and return a `persisted-with-warnings` outcome carrying a stale-base warning (coordinate the outcome shape with task 016 — do not invent a second one).</step>
    <step order="4">Client: render the warning honestly ("you overwrote a newer version — previous versions are in version history") and ADVANCE the stamp/etag state so the warning does not repeat.</step>
    <step order="5">Add a seam test: an external writer moves the base between load and save; assert the save SUCCEEDS, warns once, and a second save does not re-warn.</step>
    <step order="6">Verify no 412 remains reachable on the ContentModel path.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If removing the refusal would allow a genuine data-loss scenario that version history cannot recover (e.g. the overwritten version is never retained), STOP and surface it — the owner's last-writer-wins decision assumes SPE version history is the safety net.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs</output>
    <output type="code">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeWorkspace.tsx</output>
    <output type="test">tests/integration/seam/Compose/ComposeConcurrencySeamTests.cs</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/etag-vs-ctag-finding.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>A save onto a moved base SUCCEEDS and returns a warning outcome — no 412 refusal on the ContentModel path.</criterion>
    <criterion>The warning names SPE version history as the recovery path.</criterion>
    <criterion>An immediate second save after a warned save does NOT re-warn for the same event (loop impossible) — proven by test.</criterion>
    <criterion>The eTag-vs-cTag assessment is recorded with evidence.</criterion>
    <criterion>Staleness DETECTION still exists — only the refusal is removed.</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: a save with an UNMOVED base produces no warning (no false positives).</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: no IMemoryCache used for version state (ADR-009).</criterion>
    <criterion>Publish size reported vs 44.96 MB; no new HIGH CVE; Placement Justification stated in the PR.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
